In Go 1.21+, you can use the standard `slices` package to sort slices conveniently:

```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "slices"
)

func main() {
    numbers := []int{5, 2, 6, 3, 1, 4}
    slices.Sort(numbers)
    fmt.Println(numbers) // Output: [1 2 3 4 5 6]
}
```

For custom structs, use `slices.SortFunc` with a comparator function:
```go
slices.SortFunc(users, func(a, b User) int {
    return cmp.Compare(a.Age, b.Age)
})
```

Goroutines are lightweight threads managed by the Go runtime. Channels provide typed conduits through which goroutines synchronize and communicate:

```go
func worker(ch chan string) {
    ch <- "work complete"
}

func main() {
    ch := make(chan string)
    go worker(ch)
    msg := <-ch
    fmt.Println(msg)
}
```

Buffered channels `make(chan T, cap)` allow asynchronous non-blocking sends until the buffer fills.
